","Chronic stress can impair the function of natural killer (NK) cells in the immune system. NK cells are a type of white blood cell that play a crucial role in the immune system by recognizing and destroying abnormal cells, such as cancer cells and virus-infected cells. They do this without prior activation by antigens, making them a key part of the innate immune response. Chronic stress can lead to a decrease in NK cell activity, increasing susceptibility to infections and potentially promoting cancer progression.

