To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we use the formula:

ΔH = Σ ΔH₀(products) - Σ ΔH₀(products)

where ΔH₀ is the standard enthalpy of formation.

For the given reaction:

\[
\text{CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(H)(OH)₂(s)}
\]

The products are Ca(H)₂(O)₂(s), and the reactants are CaO(s) and H₂O(l).

The standard enthalpy of formation for:
- \( \text{CaO(s)} = -635.1 \, \text{kJ/mol} \)
- \( \text{H}_2\text{O}(l) = -285.8 \, \text{kg/mol} \)
- \( \text{Ca(H)}_2\text{(O)}_2\text{(s)} = -986.2 \, \text{kg/mol} \)

