","The system reliability function \( R_S(t) \) is the probability that the system is still operational at time \( t \). For non-repairable systems, the components do not recover from failures.  

**a) Serial System**  
In a serial system, the system fails if any component fails. The system is only operational if all components are operational.  

If the system consists of \( n \) components with independent failure rates \( \lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3, \), \( \lambda_n \), and failure rates \( \lambda_i \) are assumed to be constant, the probability of survival of each component at time \( t \) is \( P_i(t) = 1 - \lambda_i t \).  

The system reliability function is the product of the survival probabilities of each component:  
\[ R_S(t) = P_1(t) \times P_2(t) \times P_3(times) \times P_n(t) = (1 - \lambda_1 t) \times (1 - \lambda_2 t) \times (1 - \lambda_3 t) \times (1 - \lambda_n t) \]  
For \( n \) components, the general formula is:  
\[ R_S(t) = \prod_{i=1}^n (1 - \lambda_i t) \]  
If the components have the same failure rate \( \lambda \), then:  
\[ R_S(t) = (1 - \lambda t)^n \]  

**b) Parallel System**  
In a parallel system, the system is functional if any one component is functional. The system fails only if all components fail.  

The system reliability is the probability that all components survive at time \( t \), which is the product of the survival probabilities of each component.  
\[ R_S(t) = P_1(t) \times P_2(t) \times P_3(t) \times P_n(t) = (1 - \lambda_1 t) \times (1 - \lambda_2 t) \times (1 - \lambda_3 t) \times (1 - \lambda_n t) \]  
For \( n \) components, the general formula is:  
\[ R_S(t) = \prod_{i=1}^n (1 - \lambda_i t) \]  
If the components have the same failure rate \( \lambda \), then:  
\[ R_S(t) = (1 - \lambda t)^n \]  

**Note:** The formulas for serial and parallel systems are the same when the components have the same failure rate.  

**c) Combination of Serial and Parallel Systems**  
For a combination of serial and parallel structures, the reliability function is calculated by breaking down the system into its components and subsystems.  

