</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the entropy change (\(\Delta S_{rxN}\)) for the given reaction, we use the formula:
\[
\Delta S_{rxN} = \sum n \cdot S_f(products) - \sum n \cdot S_f(reactants)
\]
where \(n\) is the stoichiometric coefficient and \(S_f\) is the molar entropy of the substance.

For the given reaction:
\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) \]
The molar entropies are:
\[
\Delta S_{rxN} = 2 \times S_f(PbO) + 2 \times S_f(SO_2) - 2 \times S_f(PbS) - 3 \times S_F(O_2)
\]
Substituting the given molar entropies:
\[
\Delta S_{rxN} = 2 \times 66.5 + 2 \times 248.2 - 2 \times 91.2 - 3 \times 205.1
\]
\[
\Delta S_{rxN} = 133 + 496.4 - 192.4 - 617.3
\]
\[
\Delta S_{rxN} = 629.4 - 809.7 = -179.3 \text{ J/mol K}
\]

Thus, the entropy change is \(\boxed{-179.3 \text{ J/mol K}}\).
