","To determine the nature of the solution, we compare the hydronium ion concentration \([H_3O^+]\) to the concentration of hydroxide ions \([OH^-]\). At \(25^\circ C\), the concentration of hydronium and hydroxide ions in a solution is related by the ion product of water, \(K_w\):
\[
[H_3O^+][OH^-] = K_w = 10^{-14} \, \text{M}^2
\]
For a neutral solution, \([H_3O^+] = [OH^-] = 10^{-7}\) M.
For an acidic solution, \([H_3O^+] > 10^{-7}\) M and \([OH^-) < 10^{-7}\) M.
For a basic solution, \([H_3O^+] < 10^{-7}\) M and \([OH^-) > 10^{-7}\) M.

The given concentration of hydronium ions is \(10^{-4}\) M. Since \(10^{-4} > 10^{-7}\), the concentration of hydronium ions is greater than that of hydroxide ions, and the solution is acidic.

Thus, the solution is acidic.

