\]
The number of brands for each type is:
\begin{itemize}
    \begin{itemize}
        \begin{itemize}
        \item Pure milk: 30 brands
        \item Yogurt: 10 brands
        \item Infant formula: 35 brands
        \item Adult milk powder: 25 brands
        \end{itemize
    \end{itemize
\end{item}
The proportion of each type is:
\begin{itemize}
    \begin{itemize}
        \begin{itemize}
        \item Pure milk: $\frac{30}{100} = 0.3$
        \item Yogurt: \(\frac{10}{100} = 0.1\)
        \item Infant formula: \(\frac{35}{100} = 0.35\)
        \item Adult milk powder: \(\frac{25}{100} = 0.25\)
        \end{itemize
    \end{{itemize
    \end{item}
The sample size for each type is proportional to the proportion of the type in the total number of brands.
If 7 brands of infant formula are selected, then the proportion of infant formula in the sample is \(\frac{7}{n}\).
Since the proportion of infant formula in the total population is \(\frac{35}{100} = 0.35\), the proportion in the sample should also be \(\frac{7}{n}\).
Thus, \(\frac{7}{n} = \frac{35}{100}\).
Multiplying both sides by \(n\), we get \(7 = \frac{35}{100} n\).
Multiplying both sides by \(\frac{100}{35}\), we get \(7 \cdot \frac{100}{35} = n\).
\[
n = \frac{700}{35} = 20
\]
Thus, the total sample size is \(n = 0.3 \times 20 + 0.1 \times 20 + 0.35 \times 20 + 0.25 \times 20 = 6 + 2 + 7 + 5 = 20\).
