2 \text{H}_2(g) + \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{H}_2\text{O}(l)
\]  
The standard Gibbs free energy change (\(\Delta G_0\)) for this reaction can be calculated using the standard Gibbs free energy of formation (\(\Delta G_0(H_2\text{O}\))) for each species:  
\[
\Delta G_0 = 2 \times \Delta G_0(H_2) + 1 \times \Delta G_0(O_2) - 2 \times \Delta G_0(H_2\text{O})
\]  
The standard Gibbs free energy of formation for hydrogen (\(\text{H}_2\)) and oxygen (\(\text{O}_2}\)) is 0 J/mol (since they are in their standard state). The standard Gibbs free energy of formation for water (\(\text{H}_2\text{O}\)) is given as \(-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}\).  
Substituting the values:  
\[
\Delta G_0 = 2 \times 0 + 1 \times 0 - 2 \times (-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}) = 474.2 \text{ kJ}
\]  
Since the reaction is at a constant temperature and pressure, the change in Gibbs free energy (\(\Delta G\)) is equal to \(\Delta G_0\).  
Thus, the change in Gibbs free energy is \(\boxed{474.2 \text{ kJ}}\).
