<USER_REQUEST>
Calculate the average radiation dose to the liver if 1.6 kg of liver tissue absorbs 0.12 J of energy from a radiopharmaceutical.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the average radiation dose to the liver, we use the formula:  
\[
\text{Dose} = \frac{\text{Energy absorbed}}{\text{Mass of tissue}} \times 1000 \text{ J/Gy}
\]  
where:  
- Dose is in Gy (Gray)  
- Energy absorbed is in J (Joules)  
- Mass of tissue is in kg (kilograms)  
- The conversion factor of 1000 J/Gy is used to convert energy in J to dose in Gy.  
Plugging the given values into the formula:  
\[
\text{Dose} = \frac{0.12 \text{ J}}{1.5 \text{ kg}} \times 1000 \text{ J/Gy} = 0.08 \text{ J/kg} \times 1000 \text{ J/Gy} = 0.08 \text{ Gy}
\]  
Thus, the average radiation dose to the liver is \(\boxed{0.08 \text{ Gy}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

