   - Early Conditions: In the early universe, dark matter was more abundant than ordinary matter. This allowed dark matter to form dense regions (dark matter ""lumps"") before ordinary matter could coalesce due to its lower density and the effects of quantum interactions. These dense dark matter regions could then gravitationally attract ordinary matter, creating regions of higher density where black holes could form more readily.

