A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by the force on the proton divided by its mass. The force on the proton is given by the electric field multiplied by the charge of the proton:
\[ F = q E = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N} $$
The acceleration is then:
\[ a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2 \]

(b) The proton starts from rest, so its initial velocity is \(v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s}\). The final velocity is \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\). The acceleration is \(a = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2\). We can use the kinematic equation:
\[ v = v_0 + a t \]
Since \(v_0 = 0\), we have:
\[ v = a t \]
Solving for \(t\):
\[ t = \frac{v}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.5 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{s} = 7.5 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{s} = 7.5 \mu\text{s} \]

(a) The magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is \(4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2\).
(b) The time it takes the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is \(7.5 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{s}\).

Final Answer:
(a) \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\)
