","The work done by an ideal gas during an isothermal expansion is given by the formula:
\[
W = -nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right)
\]
where:
- \( W \) is the work done by the gas
- \( n \) is the number of moles of the gas
- \( R \) is the ideal gas constant (8.314 J/mol·K)
- \( T \) is the temperature in Kelvin
- \( V_1 \) is the initial volume
- \( V_2 \) is the final volume

First, we need to find the temperature \( T \). Since the gas is an ideal gas, we can use the ideal gas law:
\[
P_1 V_1 = nRT
\]
where:
- \( P_1 = 2 \, \text{atm} = 2 \times 10100 \, \text{Pa} = 202000 \, \text{Pa} \)
- \( V_1 = 4 \, \text{L} = 4 \times 10^{-2} \, \text{m}^3 \)
- \( n = 2 \, \text{mol} \)

Substituting the values into the ideal gas law:
\[
202000 \, \text{Pa} \times 4 \times 10^{-2} \, \text{m}^3 = 2 \, \text{mol} \times 8.314 \, \text{J/mol·K} \times T
\]
\[
8080 \, \text{J} = 16.628 \, \text{J K} \times T
\]
\[
T = \frac{8080}{16.628} = 483.2 \, \text{K}
\]

Now, we can calculate the work done by the gas during the expansion:
\[
V_2 = 8 \, \text{L} = 8 \times 10^{-2} \, \text{m}^3
\]
\[
W = -nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right) = -2 \, \text{mol} \times 8.314 \, \text{J/mol·K} \times 483.2 \, \text{K} \ln \left( \frac{8 \times 10^{-2}}{4 \times 10^{-2}} \right)
\]
\[
W = -2 \times 8.314 \times 483.2 \ln (2) = -2 \times 8.314 \times 483.2 \times 0.6938
\]
\[
W = -2 \times 8.314 \times 483.2 \times 0.6938 = -1100.0 \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the work done by the gas during the expansion process is \(\boxed{-1100 \, \text{J}}\).

Final Answer:
\[
-1100 \, \text{J}
